    "summary": "Razor‑sharp Jerry, standout High Time & The Other One, rough Cosmic Charlie",
    "blurb": "The set opens with high‑energy Casey Jones and Me & My Uncle before settling into solid blues and ballads. Highlights include razor‑sharp Jerry on High Time, a soaring Easy Wind, and an intense The Other One that builds into a rough Cosmic Charlie. Tom Constanten’s last show adds quirky keyboards, especially on China Cat Sunflower.",
    "review": "On January 30 1970 at the Warehouse in New Orleans, the Dead delivered a performance that captures the transitionary spirit of the early ’70s lineup. Jerry Garcia was in top form – his leads were razor‑sharp, with perfect tone and atmospheric solos that lifted songs like High Time and Easy Wind to new heights. The band’s chemistry shone through the bluesy stretches of Black Peter and the gentle swing of China Cat Sunflower, where Tom Constanten’s final appearance added a uniquely muddled yet bright texture. The second half built momentum into an expansive The Other One, though the segue into Cosmic Charlie felt awkward and a bit rough‑cut, it still retained the raw power that defines this era.\n\nThe soundboard capture (gd70-01-30.sbd.vernon.10546.sbeok.shnf) conveys the energy well despite minor imperfections in the latter transition. With solid overall audio quality, this recording is the definitive way to experience a night where Jerry’s guitar work was on fire, Tom’s keyboards gave a historic farewell, and the rhythm section kept the groove flowing from start to finish.",
